    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our lineup leans heavily into favorable matchups against vulnerable arms to capitalize on our offensive output. **Corbin Carroll** (80.29) is the clear standout, as his elite sprint speed and prime position in the #2 hole against Michael Lorenzen make him a must-start; the efficiency score is buoyed by a strong park factor and a favorable platoon matchup. Following closely, **Brice Turang** (74.07) provides the secondary speed floor we covet, benefiting from a soft matchup against Roki Sasaki. While **Josh Naylor** (69.12) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.05) aren't traditional speed threats, their high-floor projections against Kolek and Lucas Giolito, respectively, allow us to anchor the heart of our order with reliable production, ensuring we maximize our total base and RBI potential in those specific slots.\n\nThe bench decision process remains rigorous, with **Kyle Karros** (51.32) serving as the most difficult omission. While his projection against Zac Gallen is respectable, he ultimately fell short because the optimizer prioritized the platoon-neutral efficiency of **Austin Riley** (66.60), whose performance is currently boosted by a favorable park factor and a situational edge against the slower delivery speed of his opponent. Elsewhere, we are monitoring the status of **Dalton Rushing** and **Gabriel Moreno**, both of whom are currently listed as \"Lineup Pending.\" Should **Dalton Rushing** be pushed further down the order or benched entirely, we will have to pivot, though his current projected efficiency against Robert Gasser suggests he remains a viable option. We are also factoring in the unfortunate absence of **Jose Caballero**, who remains benched due to a rainout, and our IL-bound veterans **Mickey Moniak** and **Trevor Story**, who are unavailable to provide any late-game relief. All eyes are on the weather reports for the early matchups to ensure our optimal lineup holds throughout the day.",
